---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Calculator from "../components/Calculator";
---

<Layout>
	<main class="mx-auto max-w-5xl w-full px-6 py-10">
		<div class="mb-8 max-w-2xl">
			<p class="section-eyebrow">Predict race times across distances</p>
			<h1 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight mt-1 mb-3">
				Estimate a runner's times from one known result
			</h1>
			<p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed">
				Enter one race time and the calculator scales it to neighbouring
				distances using special-endurance coefficients measured across elite
				athletes. Chain several distance pairs to reach further up or down the
				ladder.
			</p>
		</div>

		<section class="overflow-hidden calculator-panel">
			<Calculator client:load />
		</section>

		<section class="mt-12 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 max-w-4xl">
			<div>
				<div class="mb-4">
					<p class="section-eyebrow">Method</p>
					<h2 class="text-[15px] font-semibold mt-1">How the estimate works</h2>
				</div>
				<p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed">
					Every time is reduced to a pace per 100&nbsp;m, scaled by the
					coefficient for the chosen distance pair, then expanded to the target
					distance. Coefficients always act on the 100&nbsp;m pace, never on the
					raw time.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="mb-4">
					<p class="section-eyebrow">Range</p>
					<h2 class="text-[15px] font-semibold mt-1">Reading the min–max band</h2>
				</div>
				<p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed">
					The band is the widest plausible spread from the study's extremes, not
					a statistical confidence interval. Chaining more pairs compounds those
					extremes, so longer extrapolations show a wider band by design.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
	</main>
</Layout>
